## Model rationale

### MiMo-V2.5 as primary workhorse (restored 2026-08-23)

- **Cost**: ~$0.0004/request average on Go plan (30,100 req/5h quota)
- **Speed**: fast time-to-first-token vs ox-alpha-free (which had always-on CoT adding ~3-5s latency per response)
- **Zero data-training**: does NOT train on user data (unlike Muse Spark Contributor, Big Pickle, Hy3-free, Nemotron-free)
- **Weakness**: Akita 67/B (vs Flash 78/B, GLM-5.2 92/A.1) — the OMO harness compensates. Independently verified weaker than Flash on raw benchmarks but the speed + cost + zero-training tradeoff is favorable for volume work
- **Fallback chain**: cheap→expensive gradient; mimo-v2.5 ($0.0004) → gpt-5.6-luna ($0.0015) → deepseek-v4-flash-free ($0) → minimax-m3 ($0.0037)

### Ox Alpha retired from routing (2026-08-23)

- **Reason**: consistently slow time-to-first-token across all agents. Always-on CoT (reasoning tokens before output) added significant latency (~3-5s overhead) that made the agent feel sluggish vs mimo-v2.5
- **Status**: removed from model field and fallback chains entirely. modelConcurrency dropped to 5 (was 10)
- **If free window ends or model is deprecated**: fallback auto-catches via mimo-v2.5 primary

### GLM-5.2 → quality fallback only

- Akita 92/A.1 (independently verified), BenchLM coding 88th pct, strong agentic
- Best quality-per-quota at 880 req/5h, $60/mo
- Sits as fallback on reasoning/planning agents (oracle, prometheus, metis, momus, ultrabrain)
- Not primary — expensive and lower quota than mimo-v2.5

### GPT 5.6 Luna on code-gen

- Budget frontier (SWE-Pro 62.7, coding rank #6, $0.20/$1.20 — 1/12 of Max's input price, 2,050 req/5h)
- **Luna is served via OpenAI `/responses`, not chat/completions** — works only through opencode's BUILT-IN opencode-go provider
- Also the top-level `opencode.jsonc` default model
- review/test-writer stay on Luna exclusively

### MiniMax M3 only on visual-engineering

- The one slot that genuinely needs image/video input
- Agentic numbers are vendor-only; BenchLM-independent ranks it weak (#96/128 agentic, 40.5/100)
- flash 0731 dominates (TB2.1 82.7 vs 66.0) at 2.7× less — M3 kept only for multimodal input

### DeepSeek V4 Flash effectively retired from primary routing

- `opencode-go/deepseek-v4-flash` appears ONLY on the two DISABLED agents (atlas, hephaestus)
- Free-tier `deepseek-v4-flash-free` / `mimo-v2.5-free` remain as fallback tail — no primary routing

### fallback_models allowlist (2026-08-02/03, claude-opus-5 incident)

Every agent/category carries a allowlist suppressing OMO's HARDCODED AGENT_MODEL_REQUIREMENTS chain (sisyphus chain[0] was claude-opus-5/anthropic — fired on transient errors despite disabled_providers; see docs/INCIDENTS.md). Pattern: `fallback_models[0]` = primary model, tail = gradient from cheap to expensive.

### Budget layering

Three enforcement layers (deliberate, not a conflict): soft routing target ~$15/mo (falsification trigger above) → hard block via routing + `modelConcurrency` caps (per-model concurrency zeros out budget-blocked models; qwen3.7-max concurrency=0 since 2026-08-02) → monitoring ceiling $60/mo.

## NOT-list (do not do)

1. **NEVER add a custom `opencode-go` provider entry with `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`** — it breaks gpt-5.6-luna (responses-only transport). The built-in opencode-go provider handles per-model transport.
2. No Kimi K3 ($3/$15), no Grok 4.5 ($2/$6) — spend stays below that tier.
3. No Qwen3.7 Max ($2.50/$7.50) — hallucination-prone (Akita v2 51/C); concurrency 0.
4. No Muse Spark 1.2 Contributor as PRIMARY unless you accept data-training — it trains on prompts/completions (the discount). Reserve for fallback only.
5. Routing scope only — no permission/hook/agent-definition/provider changes.
6. No non-opencode-go / non-deepseek providers.
7. No `opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro` — still the Apr preview; not worth the spend.
8. **Hy3: SKIP** (input $0.14 = MiMo but output $0.58 2×, quota 4,300/5h) — no edge.
9. **Ox Alpha: SKIP** — too slow (always-on CoT latency). Removed from routing entirely.

## Refresh trigger

Lightweight periodic re-review (LANDSCAPE-lite): when the user senses the
landscape shifted, or a model is added/retired on opencode.ai/docs/go, or
falsification signals appear (frontier outputs no measurable gain ~2 weeks, main
agent 429-starvation, spend >$15/mo). Re-verify against the live model list
(https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/models) and models.dev before changing anything.

## Benchmark integrity (2026-08-03)

When comparing models during LANDSCAPE checks, cite ONLY contamination-resistant
benches. **SWE-bench Verified is DEAD as a signal** — OpenAI Frontier Evals
stopped reporting it (Apr 2026): audit found >60% of tasks unsolvable-as-written,
models reproduce gold patches VERBATIM from task ID, saturated at 93.9%;
any score >80% warrants harness/tool-access scrutiny. Do NOT cite it.

Trustworthy: SWE-bench Pro (contamination-resistant by design — GPL-copyleft
+ private repos, held-out/commercial subsets; frontier ~59% on Pro vs 80-95%
on Verified), LiveCodeBench (time-split, contamination-free by design), HLE +
PaperBench (novel/fail-safe design). Caveat-only: Terminal-Bench 2.1 (canary
"largely symbolic safeguard" per its own paper; interactive-CLI harder to game
but vendor-reported numbers like flash 0731's 82.7 need independent confirm),
BenchLM (aggregate), NL2Repo/Cybergym/Toolathlon/DSBench/DeepSWE (vendor-reported).
